WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.720
- But with that, I'd like to
invite up Susan Gibbons who,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.930
as you've heard me say, is both

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.660
university librarian and deputy,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.450
she's a Steven F. Gates '68
University librarian and

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.853
Deputy Provost for Scholarly
Communications and Collections,

00:00:18.853 --> 00:00:22.614
who is going to give a closing remarks.

00:00:22.614 --> 00:00:25.700
- And actually, I have a third
hat that I'm wearing today,

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.470
which is that I am the point person

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.920
for the Yale-Smithsonian
partnership here at Yale,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.200
and have the honor of doing that.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.610
So, what I wanted to say first is

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.880
thank you all for participating in this.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.360
For those who have traveled
up from DC and elsewhere,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.957
we really appreciate you coming.

00:00:42.957 --> 00:00:45.970
This has been an experiment
for us, so this is,

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.977
we are now into our
third, almost fourth year,

00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:51.650
of the Yale-Smithsonian Partnership.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.590
And our question was, while
we've had this partnership,

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.704
and certainly connections have been made,

00:00:57.704 --> 00:01:01.270
are there other ways that we
can really foster that network

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.580
and help to push it out?

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.510
And so the idea was,
could we pick one topic,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.622
in this case Michigan learning
across cultural heritage,

00:01:09.622 --> 00:01:12.974
and bring that, bring individuals together

00:01:12.974 --> 00:01:15.350
to talk about projects
that were happening?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.660
And then from that,
hopefully create networks,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.510
and from those networks maybe
projects that build on it.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.490
So this is an experiment of ours to say,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.790
how can we keep, we have the partnership

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.590
but we want to really push on it.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.200
We really want to find ways that

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.230
the partnership can,
eventually, create many more

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.260
collaborations and projects together.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.564
So hopefully, you will have
found this to be useful.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:42.235
What we're looking for are
what are the other topics?

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:46.030
There are vast numbers, and
our institutions are so vast

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.560
that it's hard to make those connections.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.400
So what we need are other
ideas of this kind of theme

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.550
that we could then, with that theme,

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.550
find out who across the two institutions

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.109
should be brought together, share ideas,

00:01:57.109 --> 00:01:59.600
and then start a network in that area.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.640
So I hope you'll think about
that in the days to come,

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.580
and give us some ideas
of how we might come up

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.638
with other themes that
really would have helped

00:02:08.638 --> 00:02:11.080
to foster this partnership
and move it forward.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.500
So I just want to end by
saying a great thanks to

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.890
Peter Leonard who has served as

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.423
our wonderful host today and facilitator,

00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:23.150
the members of Yale's
Digital Humanities Lab

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.825
who also served as your hosts,

00:02:24.825 --> 00:02:27.647
the Yale-Smithsonian advisory committee,

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:31.500
as well as Michelle Delaney who
was our key point of contact

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.661
at the Smithsonian, who
really helped to figure out,

00:02:33.661 --> 00:02:36.700
we have someone at Yale who's
interested in this area,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.380
is there someone at the
Smithsonian, and visa-versa.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.960
And so she helped with a
lot of the match-making

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.490
that brought all of you
together in this room.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.680
So to everybody who was involved today,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.270
thank you very much, we
do want that feedback.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.990
Should we do more of these,
what are the themes for them?

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.040
And then I hope you'll
take up Peter's offer

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.659
to really keep the dialogue
coming in this particular

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:00.740
domain space and continue to think about

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.430
potential collaborations and partnerships.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.820
So thank you very much, and
safe travels to all of you,

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.762
and stay dry, hopefully
this storm will go away.

00:03:08.762 --> 00:03:11.012
(clapping)

00:03:15.062 --> 00:03:17.562
(Light music)

